  "body": " Computing random forest splits for classification   In this exercise, we will practice computing the splits made by a random forest model in a two-dimensional predictor space for a classification problem. The provided figure shows the predictor plane defined by and , the data points, and the dashed lines that represent potential split locations.  The split error for classification is the weighted average of the errors for the left and right nodes, where the weights are the proportions of observations in each node. The formula for the error is given below for split . where is the number of points in the parent node, and are the number of points in the left and right child nodes, respectively. and are the classification errors for the left and right child nodes, respectively.   Qualitative data (three circles and three triangles) on a two-dimensional predictor space, X1 and X2. A non-uniform grid of all potential split locations is shown as dashed lines.      Using recursive binary splitting with tree depth of 2, how many errors corresponding to possible splits will you need to compute?    You will need to compute the errors for 4 rows in the table to determine the first split.  Next, you will need to compute 5 more errors for splitting one of the child nodes along , and at most 3 more errors for splitting the other child node along . So altogether, you will need to compute at most 12 errors to determine the first and second splits. This is because each split divides the data into two child nodes, and you will need to evaluate the potential splits for each child node separately.      Using recursive binary splitting, draw the splits corresponding to tree depth of 2 using as many rows of the randomly generated sequence as needed to decide the predictor to split on.  Table provides a starting point for you, but you must append additional rows to the table for the additional splits.  The split variable and split point for each split must be determined by the split that minimizes the split error .   Starting table for classification error rate versus splits. Add more rows as needed.    Splits              Work in teams on this problem.    "
